"I\u0027ve recorded the investigation with ReportInvestigation. I changed no code.\n\n**Cause:** \u0060OrderApi.GetOrderTotal\u0060 in \u0060src/OrderService/Api/OrderTotal.cs\u0060, line 18, does \u0060DiscountRates[order.DiscountCode]\u0060. Order 1003 carries \u0060SUMMER25\u0060, which is not in the table (only \u0060WELCOME10\u0060 and \u0060VIP20\u0060 are), so the indexer throws \u0060KeyNotFoundException\u0060. Area: **code**. Confidence: **high**.\n\n**Is the lookup the only cause?** Yes, on this path. I checked the stack, the data and the related code:\n- Line 18 is the throwing line. Order 1003 exists in \u0060data/orders.csv\u0060 with \u0060SUMMER25\u0060, and \u0060Subtotal\u0060 and the repository lookup both work for it.\n- No other place in the repository contains \u0060SUMMER25\u0060.\n- The error is not a data fault: a retired code on an order is the situation the runbook describes. The code should tolerate it.\n\n**Would the fix change totals for valid codes?** No, if the fix is a \u0060TryGetValue\u0060 with a rate of 0 when the code is missing. \u0060WELCOME10\u0060 and \u0060VIP20\u0060 would still resolve to 0.10 and 0.20, the rounding stays the same, and orders with no code still return early with the subtotal. For order 1003 the subtotal is 3 \u00D7 8.00 \u002B 2 \u00D7 5.50 = 35.00, so the expected total is 35.00 instead of the exception. The existing tests (\u0060GetOrderTotal_AppliesTheDiscountCode\u0060 for 1001 and \u0060GetOrderTotal_WithoutDiscount_IsTheSubtotal\u0060 for 1002) should keep passing. I did not run them.\n\n**Related issue I noticed but did not check further:** \u0060GetDiscountLabel\u0060 in \u0060Api/DiscountLabel.cs\u0060 does not use the table. For \u0060SUMMER25\u0060 it would show \u0022SUMMER (25% off)\u0022, which is wrong for a retired code. It does not throw for this order, so it is outside the reported error, but the same fallback is worth considering there.\n\n[Answered by triage.investigator, did: ReadWorks